  "glosses": [
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-2-grammar",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 8,
      "content": "ذٰلِكَ is a distal demonstrative: “that.” الكِتٰبُ is nominative (the subject); لا رَيبَ is the “lā of absolute negation” construction, “no doubt.”"
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-3-grammar",
      "start": 9,
      "end": 16,
      "content": "Relative clause: الَّذينَ introduces “those who.” Present-tense verbs express continuing practice. بِالغَيبِ and بِما use bi- (“in / in what”)."
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-4-grammar",
      "start": 17,
      "end": 28,
      "content": "Passive أُنزِلَ means “was sent down.” The paired clauses contrast revelation sent to you with that sent before you."
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-5-grammar",
      "start": 29,
      "end": 37,
      "content": "عَلىٰ هُدًى literally “upon guidance.” المُفلِحونَ is an active participle/plural: those who attain success."
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-6-grammar",
      "start": 38,
      "end": 48,
      "content": "إِنَّ adds emphasis (“indeed”). The interrogative ءَأَنذَرتَهُم is joined to أَم (“or”), forming “whether you warn them or not.”"
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-7-grammar",
      "start": 49,
      "end": 62,
      "content": "خَتَمَ (“sealed”) is a perfect verb. عَلىٰ قُلوبِهِم / سَمعِهِم / أَبصارِهِم repeat the prepositional phrase for emphasis."
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-8-grammar",
      "start": 63,
      "end": 73,
      "content": "مَن يَقولُ is “one who says.” وَما هُم بِمُؤمِنينَ uses bi- to intensify negation: “they are not at all believers.”"
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-9-grammar",
      "start": 74,
      "end": 83,
      "content": "The Form III verb يُخٰدِعونَ conveys attempted mutual/deceptive action. إِلّا marks the exception: “only themselves.”"
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-10-grammar",
      "start": 84,
      "end": 96,
      "content": "فى قُلوبِهِم مَرَضٌ is a nominal clause, literally “in their hearts [is] a disease.” فَزادَهُم links the consequence: “so God increased them.”"
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-11-grammar",
      "start": 97,
      "end": 107,
      "content": "The passive قيلَ is “it was said.” لا تُفسِدوا is a negative command: “do not cause corruption.”"
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-12-grammar",
      "start": 108,
      "end": 114,
      "content": "أَلا is an attention particle (“surely / behold”). هُمُ appears twice for emphatic identification: “they—the corrupters.”"
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-13-grammar",
      "start": 115,
      "end": 134,
      "content": "كَما means “just as.” The question أَنُؤمِنُ is rhetorical. السُّفَهاءُ is a broken plural, “the foolish.”"
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-14-grammar",
      "start": 135,
      "end": 150,
      "content": "لَقُوا means “met”; خَلَوا إِلىٰ means “went aside to.” إِنّا and إِنَّما both provide emphasis/restriction."
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-15-grammar",
      "start": 151,
      "end": 157,
      "content": "يَستَهزِئُ and يَمُدُّهُم are imperfect verbs, depicting ongoing response. طُغيٰنِهِم is “their transgression.”"
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-16-grammar",
      "start": 158,
      "end": 168,
      "content": "اِشتَرَوا (“they bought”) is a commercial metaphor. فَما رَبِحَت تِجٰرَتُهُم: “their trade did not profit.”"
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-17-grammar",
      "start": 169,
      "end": 185,
      "content": "مَثَلُهُم كَمَثَلِ opens a simile: “their likeness is like the likeness.” فَلَمّا introduces the result after “when.”"
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-18-grammar",
      "start": 186,
      "end": 191,
      "content": "صُمٌّ بُكمٌ عُمىٌ are plural descriptive nouns: “deaf, dumb, blind.” فَهُم links the conclusion."
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-19-grammar",
      "start": 192,
      "end": 211,
      "content": "كَصَيِّبٍ is “like a rainstorm.” مِنَ الصَّوٰعِقِ (“from the thunderclaps”) expresses the cause of fear."
    },
    {
      "id": "baqarah-2-20-grammar",
      "start": 212,
      "end": 239,
      "content": "يَكادُ (“almost”) governs the following imperfect verb. كُلَّما means “whenever.” لَو (“if”) presents a counterfactual condition."
    }
  ]
}
